  "name": "West Side Sound (San Antonio Chicano R&B)",
  "family": "rock",
  "lineage": "Rhythm-and-blues, soul and rock-and-roll scene of San Antonio from the late 1950s, in which Mexican-American groups such as Sunny & the Sunliners played alongside Anglo musicians such as Doug Sahm and Augie Meyers, mixing horn-driven R&B and doo-wop-style ballads with conjunto, polka and country. Sunny & the Sunliners' 'Talk to Me' reached the national charts in 1963, and Sahm and Meyers carried the scene's Vox-organ and horn-section sound into the Sir Douglas Quintet.",
  "recipe": "classic North American 1957-1959 west side sound, functional Song, soul R&B branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment. single coil electric guitar, combo organ, tenor ensemble saxophone, bach stradivarius 180 canonical American orchestral ensemble trumpet, pocket fingerstyle locked electric bass, bronze bell bronze machine hammered symmetric pattern organized rows drum kit. dry but alive 1948-1965 indie-commercial early tape us small. passive ribbon, tube pre, colored vintage console, vari-mu, Pultec passive program EQ. Studer 30ips tape echo plate reverb.",
